00:00:19.522 --> 00:00:21.230
When you're building
a cloud application,

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.300
security is always on your mind.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.220
It takes a constant
effort to stay ahead

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.110
of the threat vectors,
which can easily

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.090
burn up engineering time.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.580
Thankfully, serverless
offerings can help cut down

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.530
the amount of effort you need.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.350
Vulnerabilities can exist in
almost every stage of a tech

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.810
stack, starting with
hardware all the way up

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:40.520
to your application code.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.040
With serverless, everything
below your application code

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.360
is managed by Google without
any actions needed on your part.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.240
While you're still responsible
for the security of your code,

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.380
GCP has a few tools to help
make it a little easier on you.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.500
First, Cloud Functions
has just released support

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.200
for max instances
configuration, which

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.360
allow you to reduce
your vulnerability

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.069
to both denial of
service attacks

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:06.980
as well as a hug of death,
where you might inadvertently

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:10.370
DOS your own back ends
without knowing it.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.250
There's also improved
budgets and budget alerts,

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.830
which can help prevent
against DOS attacks designed

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.440
to exhaust your resources.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.980
Next, Cloud Functions has
just rolled out support

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.516
for environment variables.

00:01:22.516 --> 00:01:23.890
So instead of
leaking credentials

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.840
by putting them
directly in your code,

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.020
you can manage them from a
separate console interface.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.560
And finally, new IAM
controls grant the ability

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.600
to specify which
users have access

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.170
to run an interface with
what Cloud Functions, meaning

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.870
you can create microservices,
which instead of being

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:43.210
available to the outside
world by default,

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.820
can be limited to only
interact with other systems

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.080
inside your application.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.380
Now this is just a sampling
from the main talk.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.780
If you're interested in a
larger list of vulnerabilities

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.140
